# Line endings are decided here, not by each contributor's core.autocrlf.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true at install time, so a Windows clone
# gets CRLF working files and commits LF back -- fine, until someone has it set
# to false and commits CRLF into a repository everyone else reads as LF. The
# result is a diff that touches every line of a file nobody edited.
#
# This matters more here than in most projects: the load-bearing test asserts the
# rendered EPUB's text is *byte-identical* to the original. Test fixtures are
# compared as bytes, so a stray \r in a tracked XHTML fixture is not a cosmetic
# problem, it is a failing invariant that looks like a rendering bug.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never normalize what is not text. The EPUB fixtures are zips, and an EPUB whose
# bytes git has "helpfully" adjusted is a corrupt archive.
*.epub binary
*.kepub.epub binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.png binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
